window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][65] = "<h1>Appendix A. Google product briefs [as of 2026-10]</h1>\n<p><strong>What this is.</strong> Eight one-page briefs on Google products, built with the method in C33.10 from public sources read in October 2026. They give you items 1 to 5 of the brief: the product and its users, how it fits the business, the metrics the company reports, where it stands, and recent moves. They deliberately leave out item 6 (your angle) and item 7 (your questions), because those must come from your own use of the product. Each brief ends with prompts to start from.</p>\n<p><strong>These briefs are date-sensitive.</strong> They describe products, fees, court cases, rules and figures as they stood in October 2026. Check each fact yourself at the time you read this book; the most perishable are the court schedules (A.1), the app store fees (A.6) and the payment shares and rules (A.8).</p>\n<p><strong>How to use it.</strong> Read a brief, then use the product for a week, then write your own angle. A brief that you recite is worth little in a loop. A brief that you test against your own use is worth a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>Labels.</strong> <code>Public</code> marks a statement from Google or Alphabet (a filing, a results release, a blog post by the company or its leaders). A figure marked &quot;as reported&quot; is one that the press attributes to a Google executive and that I did not find on a Google page. <code>Inferred</code> marks my reasoning, or what candidates and commentators say. Every figure carries the date it was checked. Check again before you rely on one (see the box &quot;Dates to check&quot; in C01).</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-0-the-business-in-one-table\"></span>\n<h2>A.0 The business in one table</h2>\n<p>Alphabet's results for the quarter ended 30 June 2026 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet Form 10-Q and results release, 2026]</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line</th>\n<th>Revenue, quarter ended 30 June 2026</th>\n<th>Change on the year</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Google Search &amp; other</td>\n<td>$63.3 billion</td>\n<td>up 17%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YouTube ads</td>\n<td>$11.1 billion</td>\n<td>up 13%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google subscriptions, platforms and devices</td>\n<td>$12.9 billion</td>\n<td>up 15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google Cloud</td>\n<td>$24.8 billion</td>\n<td>up 82%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alphabet in total</td>\n<td>$119.8 billion</td>\n<td>up 24%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Search and other was a little over half of Alphabet's revenue that quarter (63.3 of 119.8, my arithmetic). The growth in subscriptions, platforms and devices came mainly from paid subscriptions across YouTube services and Google One, according to the 10-Q. The Gemini app had 950 million monthly active users, according to the results release.</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-1-google-search\"></span>\n<h2>A.1 Google Search</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> Search now offers AI Overviews and AI Mode as one experience. Google's CEO said on the Q2 2026 call that AI Mode passed 1 billion monthly active users after its global expansion in October 2025 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, CEO remarks on the Q2 2026 call]</code>. AI Overviews was reported at more than 2 billion monthly users in July 2025 <code>Public</code> as reported <code>[V: trade press on the Q2 2025 call]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Search is Alphabet's largest line (A.0). Alphabet reports its monetization as changes in paid clicks and cost per click, not as a price <code>[V: Alphabet 10-Q]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> Google says AI features are adding queries and sending &quot;billions of clicks to websites every week&quot; <code>Public</code>. Publishers and independent researchers dispute how many clicks AI answers displace <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: one 2026 statistics review; not tested here]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): queries per user, the share of queries answered without a click, revenue per query, and latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> A US court found in 2024 that Google held an illegal monopoly in search. In September 2025 the judge declined to order a sale of Chrome, required Google to share some search index and user-interaction data with rivals (not advertising data), and barred exclusive default deals <code>Public</code> <code>[V: court remedies order, as reported by Moneylife and The Desk]</code>. Google filed a notice of appeal in January 2026, and the Justice Department and states filed their own appeals by 3 February 2026 <code>[V: Bloomberg Law; a litigation tracker for Google's date]</code>. On 5 October 2026 the parties jointly asked the D.C. Circuit to give the cross-appeals priority so that oral argument could be held as early as 9 December <code>[V: Law360, 5 October 2026 (headline and summary)]</code> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. The judge wrote that generative AI had changed the course of the case <code>[V: reported by Folio3]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent moves.</strong> AI Overviews and AI Mode merged into one Search experience <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, CEO remarks on the Q2 2026 call]</code>. AI Mode had launched in the US and India by July 2025 <code>[V: trade press on the Q2 2025 call]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> If an AI answer satisfies the user, what should Search count as success? Who pays when the click disappears? What would you ask legal and policy before you changed a default?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-2-google-maps\"></span>\n<h2>A.2 Google Maps</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> More than 2 billion people use Maps <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Associated Press report of Google's 12 March 2026 announcement]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> I found no separate Maps revenue figure in the filings or reports I read. Treat its business role as an open question to answer from your own reading (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> On 12 March 2026 Google announced two Gemini features. Ask Maps lets people ask questions in plain language and draws on information from more than 300 million places, including community reviews <code>[V: as reported from Google's blog post]</code>. It rolled out in the US and India first. Immersive Navigation is a 3D redesign of driving directions that Google called its biggest navigation upgrade in over a decade, starting in the US <code>[V: AP; Noqta summary of the blog post]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): navigation sessions completed, place-search success, and trust in recommendations.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands and recent moves.</strong> The AP report says Google believes its safeguards stop Gemini from inventing places <code>[V: AP]</code>. That claim is Google's, and it is the claim to test.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> What does a wrong answer cost in a map compared with a chat? Which users would you roll Ask Maps out to first, and why India second?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-3-youtube\"></span>\n<h2>A.3 YouTube</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> YouTube ads earned $11.1 billion in the quarter ended 30 June 2026, up 13% <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet Form 10-Q]</code>. Over 1.7 billion unique viewers watched World Cup videos on YouTube during the 2026 tournament <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet results release]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Two revenue streams: ads (A.0) and subscriptions, which Alphabet reports inside subscriptions, platforms and devices.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> As reported: YouTube's revenue across ads and subscriptions passed $60 billion in 2025 <code>[V: Alphabet Q4 2025 call, as relayed by a statistics roundup]</code>. YouTube Music and Premium had over 125 million subscribers, and paid subscriptions across YouTube and Google One reached 350 million by the first quarter of 2026 <code>[V: CEO statements as relayed by TIKR from MoffettNathanson and Alphabet's Q1 2026 release]</code>. Shorts is reported at about 200 billion daily views, and one source notes that the counting rules changed during the run <code>[V: a 2026 statistics review]</code>. I did not open YouTube's own pages for these figures. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): watch time on TV screens against mobile, Shorts revenue per hour, creator payouts.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> YouTube's CEO said it paid more than $100 billion to creators, artists and media companies over four years <code>[V: as reported]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent moves.</strong> Cheaper and genre-specific plans (a Premium Lite and genre plans for YouTube TV) were reported in May 2026 <code>[V: TIKR]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> If Shorts earns the same per hour as long video, what changes in recommendations? How would you tell a subscriber you won from one you merely moved?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-4-gmail\"></span>\n<h2>A.4 Gmail</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> About 3 billion people use Gmail <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, &quot;Gmail is entering the Gemini era,&quot; 8 January 2026]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Gmail is a reach product that anchors Google accounts, storage and subscriptions <code>Inferred</code>. Alphabet's filings I read do not break out Gmail revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> On 8 January 2026 Google released Help Me Write and Suggested Replies to everyone at no cost; Proofread and &quot;ask your inbox&quot; answers went to Google AI Pro and Ultra subscribers; and AI Inbox was a test with trusted testers <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google blog]</code>. Google's data, as reported by Fortune, was that 70% of enterprise users of Help Me Write in Docs or Gmail accepted the suggestion <code>[V: Fortune]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): acceptance of suggestions, edits after acceptance, and opt-out of smart features.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands and recent moves.</strong> The features began in US English only <code>[V: Google; Fortune]</code>. Which features are free and which are paid is a live packaging decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> What would &quot;the inbox is better&quot; mean as a number? Which Gemini feature would you keep free and which paid, and what evidence would you want?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-5-google-photos\"></span>\n<h2>A.5 Google Photos</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> More than 1.5 billion monthly users and more than 9 trillion photos and videos stored, per Google's 10th-anniversary figures of May 2025 <code>Public</code> as reported <code>[V: Ars Technica, 28 May 2025]</code>. Users run over 370 million searches in Photos each month, as reported by the same source.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Storage and editing drive paid plans. Since 2021, uploads count against the 15 GB that every Google Account shares across Photos, Gmail and Drive, and Google encourages paid Google One storage <code>[V: Ars Technica]</code>. Alphabet says growth in Google One subscriptions drives part of its subscriptions line <code>[V: Alphabet 10-Q]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> The Create tab holds the AI features: Remix and photo-to-video launched in July 2025 as experiments that ask for a thumbs-up or down <code>[V: TechCrunch, 23 July 2025]</code>. A Video Remix feature began rolling out to Google AI Plus, Pro and Ultra subscribers in July 2026 <code>[V: Thurrott]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): photos edited, searches, storage-plan upgrades, and thumbs-down rate on generated media.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> Photos began in 2015 as a spin-out from Google+ <code>[V: Ars Technica]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> What is the product's job: memory, backup or creation? Which metric would tell you a free user is about to pay for storage?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-6-android-and-google-play\"></span>\n<h2>A.6 Android and Google Play</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> Android is the operating system, and Google Play is its main app store.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Play sits in the subscriptions, platforms and devices line (A.0). Google's standard service fee has been 15% on a developer's first $1 million and 30% beyond <code>[V: Android Authority]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> Google's Play policy page says the US injunction in the Epic Games case was upheld by the Ninth Circuit on 12 September 2025, and lists compliance steps: developers may link users to outside offers from 29 October 2025, Play began sharing its catalog with third-party US app stores in July 2026, and developers in the alternative-billing programs must report transactions and pay fees from 1 October 2026 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google Play Console policy page]</code>. Rival app stores can be installed from Play in the US from 22 July 2026, with a $5,000 annual review fee for stores <code>[V: PocketGamer.biz]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): the share of installs outside Play, developer retention, and security incidents from alternative stores.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands and recent moves.</strong> Google and Epic announced a settlement in March 2026 with lower fees and a registered-app-store program outside the US, then withdrew their joint motion in July 2026 to change the injunction <code>[V: TechCrunch; PocketGamer.biz]</code>. Reports say Google began separating service fees from billing fees on 30 June 2026 and will require developer identity verification in four countries from September 2026 <code>[V: PocketGamer.biz news index]</code>. The fee structure announced in June 2026 charges a 10% service fee on the first $1 million a year, then 20% for new installs or 25% for existing installs above that, with link-out fees of 20% above $1 million and an extra 5% when a developer uses Google's billing system; it applies in the UK, the EEA and the US first, in Australia, Japan and South Korea by the end of 2026, and elsewhere by September 2027 <code>[V: MacRumors, 24 June 2026; Help Net Security, 5 March 2026 (the settlement terms)]</code> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> What do you protect about security when anyone can run a store? How would you price Play if every fee could be avoided?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-7-gemini\"></span>\n<h2>A.7 Gemini</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> The Gemini app had 950 million monthly active users and daily users tripled in the past year, and Gemini models processed about 22 billion API tokens a minute <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet results release and the CEO's Q2 2026 remarks]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> Gemini is both a product and the engine inside others. Gemini 3 powered the Gmail features in January 2026 <code>[V: Google blog]</code>, and a model called Gemini Omni was reported behind Video Remix in Photos and free AI video in YouTube Shorts <code>[V: Thurrott; Ngram, as reported]</code>. Paid tiers are Google AI Plus, Pro and Ultra <code>[V: Thurrott]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What Google reports, and what to watch.</strong> Alphabet reports usage (monthly users, tokens) and Cloud revenue, which grew 82% to $24.8 billion with a backlog of $514 billion <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet results; CEO remarks]</code>. Watch (<code>Inferred</code>): retention of monthly users, answer quality on your own tasks, and what the free tier gives away.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> Coverage names ChatGPT, Perplexity and Claude as rivals for the kind of questions people once typed into Search <code>[V: a 2026 article on the search appeal]</code>, which is why the Search and Gemini briefs belong together.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> If Gemini answers what Search used to answer, which product owns the user? How would you decide what a Gemini feature must not do?</p>\n<span id=\"app-a-a-8-google-pay-in-india\"></span>\n<h2>A.8 Google Pay in India</h2>\n<p><strong>Product and users.</strong> A UPI app that lets people pay from a bank account. UPI was piloted on 11 April 2016 with 21 banks, and the apps came to Google Play from 25 August 2016 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: NPCI product overview and press release]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How it fits.</strong> UPI apps may not charge consumers a platform fee <code>Public</code> <code>[V: UPI merchant-fee FAQs dated 15 September 2026]</code>, so an app's value is indirect: usage, trust and the user relationship <code>Inferred</code>. From 15 October 2026 a 0.4% merchant fee applies to person-to-merchant payments above ₹2,000, shared within the UPI ecosystem (see C09).</p>\n<p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> PhonePe and Google Pay together handled about 79% of UPI payments in May 2026, below 80% for the first time, with PhonePe near 46% <code>[V: NPCI data as reported by Outlook Business and Adda247]</code>. For July 2026 the two held 78.6%, with PhonePe near 46% <code>[V: NPCI data as reported by StartupFeed]</code> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. In November 2024, Reuters reported PhonePe at 47.8% and Google Pay at 37%.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> NPCI's rule caps any one third-party app at 30% of UPI volume. The deadline was extended twice and now falls on 31 December 2026 <code>Public</code> <code>[V: NPCI statement as reported by Business Today and MediaNama]</code>. Reports say Google Pay and PhonePe both exceed 30%, and that an app above the cap would have to stop onboarding new customers <code>[V: Inc42; MediaNama]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent moves.</strong> The merchant-fee framework (15 October 2026) and the NPCI cap (31 December 2026) both change what an app can earn and how it can grow <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Prompts (<code>Inferred</code>).</strong> If you could not onboard new users past the cap, where would growth come from? What product would earn money when the payment itself is free?</p>\n<span id=\"appendix-b\"></span>\n";
